WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.600
For a guy who hates every superhero, Peacemaker's&nbsp;
show is full of references to them. From marquee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:10.640
heroes like Batman and Superman to the obscure&nbsp;
Kite-Man and Bat-Mite – and even a little nod&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:14.720
to the upcoming "Black Adam" movie – these are&nbsp;
all the Easter eggs you missed in "Peacemaker."

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:18.960
Considering how dead he appeared to be by the&nbsp;
end of the movie, it's a bit of a miracle that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:23.360
Peacemaker walked away from the events of "The&nbsp;
Suicide Squad" at all. But walk away he did,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:27.040
and it turns out he kept a few souvenirs&nbsp;
from his ill-fated trip to South America.

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:30.960
After emptying the personal items he is&nbsp;
carrying during his hospital visit in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:34.480
first episode, viewers can plainly&nbsp;
see a postcard from Corto Maltese.

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:39.600
Furthermore, once Peacemaker attempts to pay his&nbsp;
taxi driver for the car ride to his dumpy trailer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:44.000
he offers him some currency from Corto Maltese,&nbsp;
hinting to the driver that it could be worth a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:48.960
million dollars. Of course, the driver isn't a&nbsp;
fool, and takes his helmet as payment instead.

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:53.120
Aside from being the setting of "The Suicide&nbsp;
Squad" film, Corto Maltese has an extensive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:57.840
history as a fictional country in the DC universe.&nbsp;
It was first introduced in Frank Miller's "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:03.920
Dark Knight Returns" comics, released in 1986.&nbsp;
Miller used Corto Maltese as a sensitive pressure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:08.800
point in the burgeoning tensions between the U.S.&nbsp;
and the Soviet Union in the ongoing Cold War.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:12.560
In that series, the Soviets maintain&nbsp;
control of the tiny South American island,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:16.320
but the U.S. supports a rebellion to&nbsp;
usurp power over the country's leadership.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:21.440
This causes Russia to fire a nuclear warhead&nbsp;
at the U.S., which Superman attempts to stop.

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:26.000
Corto Maltese is also referenced in Tim&nbsp;
Burton's "Batman" in 1989 and countless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:30.960
comic storylines. It also makes several&nbsp;
appearances in the CW's sprawling Arrowverse.

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:35.440
In the first episode of "Peacemaker,"&nbsp;
viewers who took a quick pause to view&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:38.640
all of Peacemaker's personal items at&nbsp;
the hospital may have noticed a note&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:42.800
with the name Valentina on it, along&nbsp;
with what might be a phone number.

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:48.000
DC comics fans will likely make the connection&nbsp;
to Valentina Vostok, a DC character who was once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:52.560
a member of the Doom Patrol. Under the alias&nbsp;
Negative Woman, the U.S. government uses her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:57.280
for her unique espionage capabilities, and&nbsp;
she often crosses paths with Peacemaker.

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:01.120
Where Peacemaker obtains her phone number&nbsp;
in his TV show is a bit of a mystery.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:05.440
Perhaps it occurs off-screen during the club scene&nbsp;
in "The Suicide Squad." Or maybe it's something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:10.000
he's been carrying with him for quite some time.&nbsp;
Seeing as Valentina is a Russian defector to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:14.560
United States, however, she may have interests in&nbsp;
Corto Maltese, or she could simply be an associate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:19.200
of Amanda Waller. It's anybody's guess, but&nbsp;
perhaps we'll see her in "Peacemaker" Season 2.

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:23.680
Peacemaker has a bone to pick with the&nbsp;
Justice League. He may even be envious of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:28.000
their popularity and prestige in the superhero&nbsp;
community. After all, Peacemaker often laments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:32.160
that no one likes him, but everyone loves&nbsp;
Superman. In the first episode, Peacemaker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:36.720
is made fun of by the hospital janitor, Jamil,&nbsp;
when he claims to be a superhero. After Jamil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:41.280
brings up his favorite hero, Aquaman, Peacemaker&nbsp;
offers a story he heard from a "credible" source.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:43.360
"Aquaman f—ks fish?"

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:43.860
"Yeah."

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:45.200
"I don't believe it."

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:47.760
"Guy on Twitter works for the aquarium. Said for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:50.320
50 bucks he brings him to the back so&nbsp;
he can have his way with a sturgeon."

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:54.320
During a meeting, Clemson Murn discusses&nbsp;
how the butterfly aliens take over human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:58.400
bodies — by entering through an orifice.&nbsp;
When the team jokes about John's slide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:03.200
depicting a butterfly entering a human anus,&nbsp;
Peacemaker mocks another famous alien, Superman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:07.760
for apparently having a unique fondness&nbsp;
for bodily waste. Once again, the internet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:12.960
apparently tells no lies in Peacemaker's mind.&nbsp;
Later, Peacemaker calls Batman a "jackass" who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:17.120
fails to do the job of eliminating villains&nbsp;
while arguing with his father's neighbor.

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:20.880
After appearing as a guest speaker at a&nbsp;
school as a favor to Jamil and his daughter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:24.720
one child asks Peacemaker if he ever&nbsp;
met the Flash. He claims he has and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:28.480
that the Flash is a jerk -– although&nbsp;
he uses more colorful descriptors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:32.320
Peacemaker also insinuates that Wonder&nbsp;
Woman has the hots for him. Overall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:36.960
it's pretty clear that Peacemaker has very little&nbsp;
respect for the greatest heroes in the DCEU.

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:41.520
It's no secret that Peacemaker doesn't&nbsp;
play nicely with others. He also carries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:45.600
grudges and seemingly has one against&nbsp;
the powerful DC comics villain, Eclipso,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:49.760
a powerful enemy who can absorb other&nbsp;
heroes and take control of their abilities.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:54.240
In the first episode, an image of the villain can&nbsp;
be seen on Peacemaker's dartboard in his trailer.

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:58.560
The Peacemaker has a not-so-friendly run-in&nbsp;
with the villain in the 1992 comic series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:02.880
"Eclipso" by Robert Loren Fleming and&nbsp;
Audwynn Jermaine Newman. In this series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:07.280
Amanda Waller led a group of obscure DC&nbsp;
characters known as the Shadow Fighters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:12.080
Peacemaker is a part of the team tasked with&nbsp;
bringing down Eclipso. The clash ends in disaster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:16.720
as many of the characters are killed — including&nbsp;
Peacemaker, who dies in a helicopter crash.

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:21.520
While this bit of history can't possibly exist&nbsp;
in the TV series as Peacemaker is alive and well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:25.120
James Gunn confirmed the reference&nbsp;
on Twitter. Regardless, Peacemaker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:28.240
seems keenly and begrudgingly&nbsp;
aware of Eclipso's existence.

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:32.480
In the first episode of Peacemaker,&nbsp;
Amanda Waller tasks her daughter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:36.160
Leota, with the job of planting a fake&nbsp;
Peacemaker diary in his apartment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:40.800
That way, once the inevitable result of the&nbsp;
mission is complete, a search of Peacemaker's home&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:45.200
will incriminate the hapless mercenary and likely&nbsp;
send him right back into Amanda Waller's arms.

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:50.400
The diary cover has a sticker featuring a logo&nbsp;
for the Wayne Foundation. The Wayne Foundation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:54.880
is a philanthropic entity, and Bruce Wayne's&nbsp;
means of advancing justice outside the cowl.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:58.240
He often uses the organization as&nbsp;
a soapbox to advocate for economic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:01.920
reforms that will aid the lower class&nbsp;
and help support the citizens of Gotham.

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:06.800
The Wayne Foundation has also provided much-needed&nbsp;
resources to the impoverished. The sticker on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:11.440
faux-Peacemaker diary is curious as the symbol&nbsp;
actually resembles the Wayne Enterprises logo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:16.080
seen in the Christopher Nolan trilogy of Batman&nbsp;
films, not the different logo found in the DCEU.

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:22.240
Of all characters to receive a live-action&nbsp;
reference, Bat-Mite was, perhaps, the least&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:28.080
unexpected. The fifth-dimensional imp is known in&nbsp;
the DC Comics lore as an extreme Batman fanatic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:32.640
Essentially, he's like many of us, just smaller&nbsp;
and with magical abilities. He shares the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:38.080
home dimension as Mister Mxyzptlk, a DC villain&nbsp;
who appears in a memorable episode of "Superman:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:42.160
The Animated Series," among other places.&nbsp;
However, Bat-Mite often just wants to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:45.920
the Dark Knight in action. Sometimes, he&nbsp;
even throws obstacles in the hero's way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:50.720
just to gawk at Batman's prowess. He's sometimes&nbsp;
helpful but is more often a pain in the rear.

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:55.440
When John Economos is bandaging up Peacemaker&nbsp;
in Episode 2, the two share words as Peacemaker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.440 --> 00:06:00.400
insists on mocking the tech liaison. John loudly&nbsp;
exclaims that he'd rather work with Harley Quinn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:04.800
the Weasel, or even Bat-Mite than with&nbsp;
Peacemaker. Confused, Peacemaker questions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:10.480
who Bat-Mite is. Either Peacemaker is living&nbsp;
under a rock in the DCEU world, or John simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:14.240
knows of Bat-Mite from his experience in&nbsp;
black ops missions under Amanda Waller.

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:19.600
In the second episode of "Peacemaker," John frames&nbsp;
Peacemaker's father, Auggie Smith, for the murder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:23.760
of the butterfly-bewitched assailant Peacemaker&nbsp;
was forced to kill in the first episode.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:27.680
When Detective Sophie Song sees Auggie's&nbsp;
followers kneeling before him in lock-up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:31.600
she remarks to her partner that she had thought&nbsp;
that "capes" were sent over to Arkham or Belle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:35.920
Reve. This aside indicates that super villains&nbsp;
typically receive mental health treatment with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:40.640
their prison stay — or they're thrown to Amanda&nbsp;
Waller to be utilized in her Suicide Squad.

00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:45.920
Arkham Asylum is a famous and iconic locale in&nbsp;
the DC universe. It's located in Gotham City and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.920 --> 00:06:51.040
houses many of Batman's villains deemed insane or&nbsp;
mentally unstable. Of course, villains commonly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.040 --> 00:06:55.360
escape from the maximum-security facility&nbsp;
because, well, Batman needs something to do.

00:06:55.920 --> 00:06:59.920
Belle Reve is another high-security facility&nbsp;
that contains the most hardened and powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.920 --> 00:07:05.040
metahumans. Amanda Waller is often seen&nbsp;
operating the Suicide Squad out of this prison.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:09.840
Most of the Suicide Squad volunteers vying for&nbsp;
a reduced prison sentence come from this prison.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:12.800
Detective Song's mention of both&nbsp;
facilities acknowledges that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:16.720
world contains baddies that aren't&nbsp;
fit for ordinary prison life. Perhaps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:21.200
considering that Auggie isn't just a garden&nbsp;
variety scumbag, and he's also the White Dragon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.200 --> 00:07:24.480
he should've landed in one of those&nbsp;
places for his prison stay instead.

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:29.840
"The system's overcrowded. He'll&nbsp;
have to stay here through his trial."

00:07:29.920 --> 00:07:34.160
James Gunn clearly has an affinity for the&nbsp;
lesser-known and obscure odd characters of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:40.400
DC Comics world. In Episode 3, Peacemaker mentions&nbsp;
another strange DC character known as Doll Man.

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:42.080
"Hey, Doll Man? You ever meet him?"

00:07:42.080 --> 00:07:42.800
"Doll Man?"

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:44.160
"He turns small."

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:48.240
Murn's explanation is a bit of an&nbsp;
oversimplification. Doll Man certainly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:53.120
does shrink, but when he does so he retains his&nbsp;
physical strength as if he were still full-grown.

00:07:53.120 --> 00:07:56.560
While there are multiple versions of the&nbsp;
character, the most famous iterations were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.560 --> 00:08:01.360
Darrel Dane, a scientist, and Lester Colt,&nbsp;
a special forces operator. They both gained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:05.920
the ability to shrink, although Lester's was&nbsp;
a bit more permanent, at least for a time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.920 --> 00:08:09.360
Who knows what iteration of the character&nbsp;
Peacemaker may have been referring to?

00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:13.360
Peacemaker has lived a wild&nbsp;
life. The series makes no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:17.440
secret of that, as he constantly unveils&nbsp;
eyebrow-raising tidbits about his past.

00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:21.680
When talking to Murn about the idea of allowing&nbsp;
Vigilante to join the team in Episode 4,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.680 --> 00:08:24.400
Peacemaker questions the call,&nbsp;
thinking that Vigilante might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:26.800
be a little too crazy for the mission at hand.

00:08:26.800 --> 00:08:30.960
Murn is aware of Vigilante's issues but is&nbsp;
willing to ignore them because he needs help.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:34.000
Peacemaker then makes a&nbsp;
truly surprising reference.

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:37.600
"Ever since I had a team-up with Matter-Eater Lad,&nbsp;
my sense of what's normal is a little f—ked up."

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:42.720
Matter-Eater Lad hails from the distant future. By&nbsp;
nature of his race, he can eat matter in all its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:48.400
forms, just as his name implies. Most famously, he&nbsp;
joins the Legion of Super-Heroes. At first blush,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.400 --> 00:08:53.360
the capability of eating anything might not&nbsp;
seem practical when employed for super-heroics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:58.080
However, Matter-Eater Lad is capable of using&nbsp;
his powers to neutralize devastating weaponry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.080 --> 00:09:02.720
and he's capable of eating powerful artifacts&nbsp;
that are deemed as indestructible. Humorously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.720 --> 00:09:06.800
Peacemaker remarks that he once saw the&nbsp;
character eat an entire Wendy's restaurant.

00:09:06.800 --> 00:09:08.080
"His name is Matter-Eater Lad?"

00:09:08.080 --> 00:09:10.000
"Yeah, with all that's going on,&nbsp;
why are you so interested in him?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.040 --> 00:09:12.560
Not the time to be getting distracted, Murn."

00:09:13.520 --> 00:09:17.200
In Episode 4, Auggie's elderly&nbsp;
neighbor has fun taunting Peacemaker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.200 --> 00:09:19.840
whom he believes is nothing&nbsp;
more than a fake superhero.

00:09:19.840 --> 00:09:23.760
At one point, Peacemaker is leaving his father's&nbsp;
home with a bunch of helmets and equipment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:27.920
The feisty neighbor mentions that Batman has&nbsp;
a coterie of super villains, listing Joker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.920 --> 00:09:31.600
Riddler, and Mad Hatter. He then asks why&nbsp;
Peacemaker doesn't have his own collection of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.600 --> 00:09:36.080
villains. Peacemaker exclaims that his enemies&nbsp;
are lying six feet underground, and he attacks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.080 --> 00:09:40.560
Batman's legacy for not being capable of pulling&nbsp;
the trigger on these dangerous characters.

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:42.000
"Batman doesn't kill people."

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:42.880
“Because he's a pussy!"

00:09:42.880 --> 00:09:44.640
"He's a dark creature of the knight!"

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:45.600
"He's a jackass!"

00:09:46.160 --> 00:09:49.600
Of course, everyone is familiar with&nbsp;
Batman's famous rogues' gallery.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.600 --> 00:09:53.440
Joker likely sits at the top of such a&nbsp;
gallery in most people's eyes. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.440 --> 00:09:58.400
the mention of Mad Hatter, also known as Jervis&nbsp;
Tetch, is yet another fun reference to a villain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.400 --> 00:10:02.560
that may not be widely known. This villain&nbsp;
is famous for developing a technology that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.560 --> 00:10:06.400
allows him to control others' minds when&nbsp;
he places a card or hat on their head.

00:10:07.360 --> 00:10:11.520
In the fourth episode, a fun little superhero&nbsp;
bunny figure can be seen sitting on the dash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:15.600
of Vigilante's car. This isn't simply&nbsp;
a throwaway detail included showing how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:20.080
child-like or off-the-wall Adrian is. In fact,&nbsp;
it's a reference to a fictional character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.080 --> 00:10:23.520
Ultra Bunny, who was created&nbsp;
for the DCEU by James Gunn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:26.800
as he revealed when he posted a&nbsp;
sketch of the character on Instagram.

00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:30.000
In "The Suicide Squad," Rick Flagg's&nbsp;
team is eliminated and he is taken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:33.840
captive by guerilla fighters rebelling&nbsp;
against the Corto Maltesian regime.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:38.160
However, he's given a new shirt and some gear when&nbsp;
it becomes clear that they are on the same side.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.160 --> 00:10:41.600
The shirt he wears for the remainder&nbsp;
of the film is an Ultra Bunny t-shirt.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.600 --> 00:10:45.840
Apparently, the fictional character&nbsp;
is even popular in Corto Maltese.

00:10:45.840 --> 00:10:47.760
Like "Peacemaker," the starring role of the

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:52.240
DCEU film "Black Adam" is played by a&nbsp;
former pro wrestler, Dwayne Johnson.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.240 --> 00:10:56.160
It only makes sense that serious fans get a&nbsp;
few teases during the wait for that movie.

00:10:56.160 --> 00:11:01.280
The "Peacemaker" series provides one minor but&nbsp;
clear-cut reference to Black Adam. In Episode 4,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.280 --> 00:11:06.160
Leota prods Adrian to kill Peacemaker's father,&nbsp;
Auggie, while he is imprisoned. Adrian decides&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.160 --> 00:11:10.560
to get close to the racist scoundrel the only&nbsp;
way he knows how –- by getting arrested. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.560 --> 00:11:14.560
Adrian goes to a break area for guards on&nbsp;
duty to chuck a garbage can into a window,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.560 --> 00:11:18.560
he passes a prison guard who's killing time&nbsp;
by reading a newspaper. One of the stories&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.560 --> 00:11:23.040
referenced is that of an "Intergang" potentially&nbsp;
seizing control in the nation of Kahndaq.

00:11:23.040 --> 00:11:27.600
In one version of his origin story, Black Adam&nbsp;
was born thousands of years ago in Kahndaq, a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.600 --> 00:11:32.800
fictional country not unlike Egypt from a cultural&nbsp;
standpoint. The wizard Shazam granted him powers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.800 --> 00:11:37.200
to act as a protector. However, when a villain&nbsp;
kills his family, Black Adam is enraged and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.200 --> 00:11:42.880
vows to protect Kahndaq by any means necessary,&nbsp;
including exacting bloody vengeance on criminals.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.880 --> 00:11:46.480
The wizard sealed Black Adam away to keep&nbsp;
him from causing further destruction.

00:11:47.440 --> 00:11:51.600
Kite-Man isn't a stranger to recent DC media&nbsp;
projects, as he had a humorous role in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.600 --> 00:11:55.760
animated "Harley Quinn" series vying for&nbsp;
Poison Ivy's love. As such, this reference&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.760 --> 00:11:59.840
may not be entirely lost on everyone. But&nbsp;
even if you've never heard of Kite-Man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.840 --> 00:12:04.480
you can probably figure out what his power is.&nbsp;
It’s just as ridiculous as his name implies.

00:12:05.040 --> 00:12:10.720
Kite-Man's entire superhero persona is modeled&nbsp;
around… a kite. He can fly or glide like a kite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.720 --> 00:12:15.360
and often employs kite-related weaponry. As&nbsp;
a comic book character, he isn't well known,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.360 --> 00:12:20.560
and is generally regarded as a charming relic&nbsp;
from a campier age of comic book storytelling.

00:12:20.560 --> 00:12:23.680
That said, Peacemaker at least&nbsp;
takes Kite-Man seriously.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.680 --> 00:12:27.600
In Episode 5, Peacemaker proudly displays&nbsp;
a newspaper clipping in his trailer where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.600 --> 00:12:31.840
he is hailed as a hero for apprehending&nbsp;
Kite-Man. While Peacemaker has been derided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.840 --> 00:12:35.680
during this series as being a wannabe&nbsp;
superhero, and even sometimes a villain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.680 --> 00:12:39.760
this clipping makes it appear that he was&nbsp;
once recognized as a true-blue good guy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.760 --> 00:12:44.000
Perhaps that's why he keeps a reminder of this&nbsp;
fond memory in his trailer. In the next episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.000 --> 00:12:48.240
he's seen telling school children the story of&nbsp;
when he brought down Kite-Man and turned him in.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.240 --> 00:12:52.800
Clearly, his battle with this less than nefarious&nbsp;
villain is something that gives him great pride.

00:12:52.800 --> 00:12:55.840
"Wouldn't he have crashed anyway due to gravity?"

00:13:01.440 --> 00:13:01.940
"No."

00:13:02.480 --> 00:13:04.960
Perhaps Auggie's elderly&nbsp;
neighbor was on to something.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.960 --> 00:13:07.680
Peacemaker may kill his enemies&nbsp;
now, but at one point he used to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.680 --> 00:13:11.800
operate as a straight arrow similar to&nbsp;
Batman -– a hero he seemingly despises.

00:13:12.800 --> 00:13:17.200
Midway through the series, Clemson Murn makes&nbsp;
a deal with a former comrade, Caspar Locke,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.200 --> 00:13:20.720
to help tamp down calls for re-working&nbsp;
the evidence incriminating Peacemaker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.720 --> 00:13:25.200
and Locke poses as the new police captain to&nbsp;
accomplish this. However, during Locke's initial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.200 --> 00:13:30.320
meeting with Murn in Episode 5, he references&nbsp;
the duo's past in the "jungles of Nyasir."

00:13:30.320 --> 00:13:35.200
Nyasir is another fictional country in DC lore&nbsp;
located in northern Africa. It's only been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.200 --> 00:13:39.440
referenced during one story, in which Superman&nbsp;
travels to the country to investigate a metahuman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.440 --> 00:13:43.600
among the membership of a church being suppressed&nbsp;
by the government. When the military takes part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.600 --> 00:13:48.320
in killing missionaries, the metahuman known&nbsp;
as Redemption launches a retaliatory attack.

00:13:48.320 --> 00:13:51.840
Superman comes to blows with the character,&nbsp;
and Redemption proves to be able to handle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.840 --> 00:13:56.320
himself against the Man of Steel. He can unleash&nbsp;
blasts of energy and maintain the same level of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.320 --> 00:14:00.400
strength as Superman. This could be just&nbsp;
a simple reference for fans to consume or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.400 --> 00:14:04.320
a potential narrative thread that may bear&nbsp;
fruit in the future. Only time will tell.

00:14:05.280 --> 00:14:07.920
If the Justice League exists in the DCEU,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.920 --> 00:14:12.080
surely some of the other more famous DC&nbsp;
heroes, like the Green Arrow, do as well.

00:14:12.080 --> 00:14:16.000
In Episode 8, the team sets off on their&nbsp;
noble mission to kill a cow, which is the food&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.000 --> 00:14:21.040
source of the series' butterflies. While devising&nbsp;
a plan, Peacemaker's anti-gravity helmet floats&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.040 --> 00:14:24.800
away when Leota accidentally activates it&nbsp;
with a voice command. Though frustrated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.800 --> 00:14:28.880
Peacemaker begins coming up with a new plan,&nbsp;
coming up with the idea to plant his sonic boom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.880 --> 00:14:32.960
helmet on top of the barn. He mentions shooting&nbsp;
a fishing line to the barn from the top of one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.960 --> 00:14:38.240
the trees and letting the helmet slide down. The&nbsp;
idea ends up reminding Vigilante of someone else.

00:14:38.240 --> 00:14:39.120
"Like Green Arrow?"

00:14:39.120 --> 00:14:41.040
"No, not like Green Arrow."

00:14:41.040 --> 00:14:44.000
Peacemaker, of course, has just&nbsp;
as much disdain for Green Arrow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.000 --> 00:14:48.000
as he does for every other DC superhero.&nbsp;
He even claims that Green Arrow goes to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.000 --> 00:14:52.080
Brony conventions and cosplays as&nbsp;
the rear-end of Twilight Sparkle.

00:14:52.080 --> 00:14:56.080
Leota finally says what we're all thinking by&nbsp;
observing that Peacemaker has an absurd story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:56.080 --> 00:15:00.160
for every superhero. Either Peacemaker&nbsp;
is pretty naïve and genuinely believes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.160 --> 00:15:03.200
everything he reads on the internet,&nbsp;
or he simply hates superheroes too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.200 --> 00:15:07.280
much to care about spreading false&nbsp;
rumors. Honestly, it's probably both.

00:15:07.280 --> 00:15:09.440
"Where do you get this nonsense?"

00:15:10.480 --> 00:15:11.200
"Google."

00:15:11.200 --> 00:15:12.640
"Well, it's not true."

00:15:12.640 --> 00:15:14.880
"You know more than Google?&nbsp;
Well, congratulations."

00:15:16.000 --> 00:15:19.920
Alright, this is likely one Easter egg&nbsp;
nobody missed. At the tail end of the series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.920 --> 00:15:23.840
the Justice League does arrive at the site of&nbsp;
the butterflies' clash with Peacemaker and crew,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.840 --> 00:15:28.720
but they're too late. While Wonder Woman and&nbsp;
Superman are presented as conspicuous silhouettes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.720 --> 00:15:33.760
Jason Momoa and Ezra Miller show up in the flesh&nbsp;
to reprise their roles of Aquaman and the Flash.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.760 --> 00:15:36.960
Aquaman even hilariously drops&nbsp;
an f-bomb as he dismisses the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.960 --> 00:15:39.840
rumors Peacemaker's been spreading&nbsp;
about what he does with the fish.

00:15:40.480 --> 00:15:42.320
"I'm so f–king sick of that rumor."

00:15:44.240 --> 00:15:45.840
"It's not a rumor."

00:15:45.840 --> 00:15:47.840
"F--- you, Barry."

00:15:47.840 --> 00:15:52.160
While it's obvious that Henry Cavill and Gal Gadot&nbsp;
didn't show up for this particular shoot day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.160 --> 00:15:56.560
Batman is curiously absent from the lineup&nbsp;
as well. We know that Ben Affleck is coming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:56.560 --> 00:16:00.000
back as Batman for a final performance&nbsp;
as the character in the "Flash" movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:03.040
which raises questions about why&nbsp;
he didn't show up here. Perhaps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.040 --> 00:16:06.880
Batman couldn't arrive on the scene as fast&nbsp;
as everyone else this time. Regardless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.880 --> 00:16:11.440
Peacemaker once again displays his disgust for the&nbsp;
team and doesn't even give them the time of day.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:11.440 --> 00:16:15.360
The only people happy to see the Justice League&nbsp;
in this show are apparently the folks at home.

